      "writtenOn": "2023-12-01T21:10:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`ret` is a very common name for a return code in Linux.\n\nOn an older kernel version I have checked out I get:\n```\n$ git grep -w \"ret \u003d\" | wc -l\n168991\n```\n\nI don\u0027t know that all of them are actually storing a return code, but all the lines I looked at either stored a return value it got from a function or one that it planned to return (in many cases both). I don\u0027t know of any other name that would be more familiar to a Linux kernel developer. Another name I often see in other code bases is `res`, but this is an other of magnitude less common in the Linux kernel.\n\nThis is probably an overly lengthy response to a `nit:` comment, but I don\u0027t think this name needs to be changed.",
